\section{Using This Template}
\label{using}

    \subsection{Copy, rename, gut}

    Three steps.

    \begin{enumerate}
        \item Copy this directory to \file{docs/appnotes/sources/AN00NN/} and rename the \file{.ltx} to match
              the convention in Section \ref{filename}.
        \item Edit the four front-matter lines in the preamble.  They are the only place the note's number,
              title and date appear, and everything else follows from them.
        \item Delete every section below and write the note.  Keep the revision table, the bibliography, the
              keyboard appendix if the note needs it, and the author block.
    \end{enumerate}

    The preamble you are keeping is four lines long:

\begin{lstlisting}
\documentclass[canadian]{article}
\usepackage{c47appnote}

\notenumber{032}
\noteshorttitle{Template}
\notedate{2026-07-29}
\notetitle{The Application Note Template}
\end{lstlisting}

    All four feed the masthead and the running heads, so keep the short title short: it has to sit in the
    middle of a 7.4 pt header between the number and the date.

    There is no byline in the masthead and no field for one.  The author is named once, in
    \cmd{authorblock} at the back beside the colophon, which is where a reader looks for attribution and
    licensing together.  A name under the title would only repeat it.

    \begin{note}
    Everything the design language provides comes from one file, \file{sources/style/c47appnote.sty}, and
    that file is the single copy.  Before AN0032 the same 50 lines of palette and keybox definitions were
    pasted into AN0029, AN0030 and AN0031, with a comment in each saying to consolidate them once all three
    had landed.  This is that consolidation.  Fix a macro once and every note gets the fix.
    \end{note}

    \subsection{The filename convention}
    \label{filename}

    Two names, and they do not match each other.  The source keeps the long hyphenated form, and the published
    PDF keeps the older spaced form the collection has always used.

\begin{lstlisting}
source   docs/appnotes/sources/AN00NN/
           AN-00NN-C47-R47-Application-Note-YYYY-MM-DD-XX-short-slug.ltx
PDF      docs/appnotes/
           AN00NN YYYY-MM-DD C47 R47 XX Short Title.pdf
\end{lstlisting}

    \cmd{XX} is the author's initials, and getting them wrong credits the work to somebody else.  The map:

    \begin{table}[ht]
        \caption{Author initials as they appear in the collection}
        \label{tab:initials}
        \centering
        \small
        \begin{tabular}{@{}l l p{0.46\textwidth}@{}}
            \toprule
            \hd{Initials} & \hd{Author} & \hd{Notes} \\
            \midrule
            JM  & Jaco Mostert    & The bulk of the collection \\
            DL  & Didier Lachieze & AN0021 Printing, AN0026 42S Compatibility \\
            DA  & David Abram     & AN0027 Structural conversions \\
            WGR & William Gordon Rutherdale & AN0023 Elliptic integrals \\
            NUK & Nigel UK        & AN0000 Installation \\
            DE  & David Emerson   & AN0029 First Steps, AN0030 Uncertainty, AN0031 Algebraic Numbers \\
            \bottomrule
        \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    \begin{warning}
    There are two Davids.  \cmd{DA} is David Abram and \cmd{DE} is David Emerson.  AN0030 and AN0031 were
    both filed as \cmd{DA} and had to be corrected.  Never copy the initials from a neighbouring note's
    filename; check who wrote that one first.
    \end{warning}

    A publication with more than one contributor carries every contributor's initials, in the order their
    work appears in it.  AN0023 is published as \file{AN0023 2026-04-17 C47 R47 WGR JM Elliptic Incl
    Anx1.pdf} because Annex 1 is Jaco Mostert's and the note it is bound behind is
    William Gordon Rutherdale's.  The source file keeps only the initials of the author of that source,
    so \file{AN-0023-...-WGR-elliptic.ltx} is right even though the PDF built from it says \cmd{WGR JM}.
    The two names disagreeing is not a mistake; the PDF is a bundle and the source is one part of it.
    Credit the same people, saying who wrote what, in \cmd{authors} at the back: Section
    \ref{backmatter}.

    The date in both names is the date of the revision being published, not the date the note was started.
    Bump it when you publish, and add a row to the revision table at the same time.

    \begin{warning}
    Never let the date come from \cmd{today}.  A note whose masthead is built from the build clock says
    something different every time anyone compiles it, and the published PDF then disagrees with its own
    filename.  AN0023 was published this way and its source carried a date four days off the PDF's until
    the note was moved onto this template.  \cmd{notedate} is the one place the date belongs.
    \end{warning}

    \subsection{Building}

    The style file lives one level up from the note, so \cmd{TEXINPUTS} has to point at it.  From the note's
    own directory:

\begin{lstlisting}
TEXINPUTS=../style: lualatex AN-00NN-...-slug.ltx
TEXINPUTS=../style: lualatex AN-00NN-...-slug.ltx
\end{lstlisting}

    Twice, so the table of contents, the \cmd{lastpage} count and the cross references settle.  The trailing
    colon on \cmd{TEXINPUTS} matters: it is what keeps the default search path.

    \cmd{lualatex} is required, not optional.  The package calls \cmd{RequireLuaTeX} and will stop under
    \cmd{pdflatex} or \cmd{xelatex}.

