\section{The Page}
\label{page}

    \subsection{Typography}

    Body and headings are TeX Gyre Heros, which is the metric-compatible Helvetica that ships with TeX Live.
    Using the clone rather than the system Helvetica is deliberate: every contributor gets the same page,
    including the ones on Linux and the CI runner, and nothing depends on what happens to be installed.

    The page is letter with a 1.25 in margin, which puts the measure near 75 characters.  That is the usual
    readability target, and it also leaves the keyboard maps room to sit at close to their natural width
    instead of being shrunk to fit.

    Mathematics is New Computer Modern Sans Math, also from TeX Live, so a variable in a derivation matches
    the prose around it instead of falling back to a serif face.  Compare the sum in Equation \ref{eq:lpu}
    with the sentence carrying it.  Fira Math was tried first and rejected: its minus sign is undersized, so
    $n - 1$ reads as an en dash, and its $\nu$ is indistinguishable from a $v$, which matters in any note
    that uses $\nu$ for degrees of freedom.

    One typographic accent does all the labelling: \cmd{lsc}, a letterspaced uppercase run.  It marks the
    masthead, a table head, a caption label and a callout title, so structural furniture never needs a colour
    or a rule of its own to be read as furniture.

    The display face is JuliaMono, and it is the one font the package cannot supply.  The screen and program
    boxes reproduce the calculator display and need its glyphs literally: \texttt{x⇄y}, \texttt{x²},
    \texttt{Σ+}, \texttt{√}, \texttt{≠}.  Install it from \url{https://juliamono.netlify.app}.  Without it the
    package falls back to DejaVu Sans Mono and warns during the build, and the boxes will be missing glyphs.

    \begin{table}[ht]
        \caption{The three faces and what each is for}
        \label{tab:faces}
        \centering
        \small
        \begin{tabular}{@{}l l p{0.44\textwidth}@{}}
            \toprule
            \hd{Role} & \hd{Face} & \hd{Used for} \\
            \midrule
            Body    & TeX Gyre Heros & Prose, headings, tables, captions, keybox labels \\
            Math    & NewCM Sans Math & Everything between dollar signs \\
            Display & JuliaMono      & \cmd{screen}, \cmd{prog}, \cmd{cmd}, listings: anything quoting
                                       the calculator or the source tree \\
            \bottomrule
        \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    \subsection{The palette}
    \label{palette}

    Seven colours, and two of them are spoken for.

    \begin{table}[ht]
        \caption{The palette.  \cmd{fcol} and \cmd{gcol} carry a fixed meaning and are never available for
                 anything else.}
        \label{tab:palette}
        \centering
        \small
        \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
        \begin{tabular}{@{}l c l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.44\textwidth}@{}}
            \toprule
            \hd{Name} & & \hd{Hex} & \hd{Meaning} \\
            \midrule
            \cmd{keyink}   & \swatch{keyink} & 1B1B1B & Body, headings, rules, keyboard: nearly everything \\
            \cmd{soft}     & \swatch{soft} & 6B6A65 & Furniture and captions, and anything deliberately quiet \\
            \addlinespace
            \cmd{fcol}     & \swatch{fcol} & DE6E10 & The f shift, and everything reached through it \\
            \cmd{gcol}     & \swatch{gcol} & 2C6DB5 & The g shift, and everything reached through it \\
            \addlinespace
            \cmd{menufill} & \swatch{menufill} & E9E7E2 & The fill of a soft-key label \\
            \cmd{lcdbg}    & \swatch{lcdbg} & F2F3EA & The paper colour of the display \\
            \addlinespace
            \cmd{warnink}  & \swatch{warnink} & B3341F & The one accent: a warning, and the draft markers \\
            \bottomrule
        \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    Three rules hold the design together, and they are worth stating plainly because breaking any of them is
    what makes two notes stop looking like each other.

    \emph{One.}  \cmd{fcol} orange means "reached through the f shift" and \cmd{gcol} blue means "reached
    through the g shift", everywhere, without exception.  Nothing else may use them: not a heading, not a
    rule, not a running head, not a callout.  A reader who has learned that on page 2 must not meet an orange
    mark on page 9 that means something else.  This is why the running heads are \cmd{soft} grey rather than
    the blue AN0029 through AN0031 first used.

    \emph{Two.}  A filled panel is the calculator's own screen.  \cmd{screen} and \cmd{prog} carry the
    \cmd{lcdbg} wash because they reproduce the display; tables, listings and callouts are unfilled and
    bounded by hairlines instead.  So a fill on the page always means the same thing, and a quotation of the
    calculator never looks like a quotation of a file.

    \emph{Three.}  Rules carry weight in one direction only.  1 pt closes the masthead and opens the author
    block, 0.7 pt opens and closes a table, and 0.4 pt is every other hairline: the section rules, the header
    rule, a capture frame, a listing.  No double rules and no vertical rules anywhere.

    Note what is missing.  There is no hue for a note or a tip, because their titles tell them apart and that
    is cheaper than teaching the reader two more colours.  \cmd{warnink} is the only accent, spent on the one
    callout that is a safety signal and on the draft markers of Section \ref{semantic}.  Everything else is
    keyink, \cmd{soft}, or a tint of keyink.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Keypress Notation}
\label{keys}

    This is the part of the design language worth most, because it is the part a reader learns once and then
    reads without effort.

    \subsection{The kinds of key}

    Six macros, one per way of reaching a function.

    \begin{table}[ht]
        \caption{Keypress macros}
        \label{tab:keymacros}
        \centering
        \small
        \begin{tabular}{@{}l l p{0.44\textwidth}@{}}
            \toprule
            \hd{Source} & \hd{Result} & \hd{Means} \\
            \midrule
            \lit|\key{7}|      & \key{7}      & Press that key \\
            \lit|\fkey{MODE}|  & \fkey{MODE}  & f shift, then the key whose orange label is MODE \\
            \lit|\gkey{CLK}|   & \gkey{CLK}   & g shift, then the key whose blue label is CLK \\
            \lit|\mkey{DEG}|   & \mkey{DEG}   & A soft key, named by the label the screen prints above it \\
            \lit|\fmkey{U.P}|  & \fmkey{U.P}  & f shift, then the soft key carrying U.P on its orange row \\
            \lit|\gmkey{REGR}| & \gmkey{REGR} & g shift, then the soft key carrying REGR on its blue row \\
            \lit|\lkey{f}|     & \lkey{f}     & A long press \\
            \bottomrule
        \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    On the C47 there is one physical shift key, pressed once for f and twice for g.  On the R47 there are two,
    a yellow f and a blue g.  The notation is the same on both, and a note giving C47 keystrokes need not
    caveat every line; say once which machine the keystrokes are for and point at the keyboard maps.

    \begin{warning}
    A softmenu page is eighteen items: six unshifted, then six f-shifted, then six g-shifted.  Before drawing
    a soft key, check the menu array in \file{src/c47/softmenus.c} and use \cmd{mkey}, \cmd{fmkey} or
    \cmd{gmkey} to match.  Prose like "\menu{ADV} \cmd{PGMSLV}" reads as unshifted and is wrong:
    \cmd{PGMSLV} is on the f row.  Drawing the badges on AN0030 and AN0031 caught two errors that bare
    command names had hidden.
    \end{warning}

    \subsection{Keystroke lines}

    A keystroke line is set on its own, indented, and wraps at the gaps between keys when it runs long.  Two
    forms.  \cmd{seq} carries the result the display leaves; \cmd{keys} is for a step with nothing to show
    for it yet.

\begin{lstlisting}
\seq{\key{3}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{4}\quad\key{+}}{X:\ \ 7}
\keys{\fkey{DISP}\quad\mkey{FIX}\quad\key{4}}
\end{lstlisting}

    \seq{\key{3}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{4}\quad\key{+}}{X:\ \ 7}

    \keys{\fkey{DISP}\quad\mkey{FIX}\quad\key{4}}

    Separate keys with \lit|\quad| where the reader should pause and with \lit|\ | where the keys form one
    token, as the digits of a number do.  \lit|\key{1}\key{1}| is two presses of one number;
    \lit|\key{1}\quad\key{1}| reads as two separate steps.

    Use \cmd{screen} for a display line quoted inside a sentence, like \screen{X:\ \ 7}, and keep it to one
    line.  Anything taller belongs in a \cmd{prog} block.

    \subsection{When prose stops working: the keying block}
    \label{keying}

    The inline forms above are the HP-42S manual's own style, and up to a handful of keys inside a sentence
    they are the most readable notation there is.  They stop working when the sequence is a whole program or
    a long setup: a paragraph with thirty keycaps in it reads as clutter, however carefully it is written.
    Past that length, set the sequence one action per row in a \cmd{keying} block, the keys on the left and
    what they accomplish on the right:

\begin{lstlisting}
\begin{keying}
    \step{\key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.}}{open a new program}
    \step{\fkey{PRGM}}{enter program-entry mode}
\end{keying}
\end{lstlisting}

    \begin{keying}
        \step{\key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.}}{open a new program}
        \step{\fkey{PRGM}}{enter program-entry mode}
    \end{keying}

    An empty right cell is fine where a step needs no comment.  Keep the comments to a phrase: the keys are
    the thing being read, which is why the column is set small and soft.  Section \ref{progentry} shows a
    complete program keyed in this way.

