\section{A Worked Tutorial}
\label{tutorial}

    This section is the shape a tutorial should take: state the goal, give the keystrokes, show the screen,
    say what happened and why.  The content is deliberately trivial so the pattern is visible through it.

    Add three and four, then set the display to four decimals.

    The C47 and R47 use RPN, so there is no equals key.  Type the first number, press \key{ENTER} to separate
    it from the second, type the second, then press the operation.

    \seq{\key{3}\quad\key{ENTER}\quad\key{4}\quad\key{+}}{X:\ \ 7}

    \shot{02-result}{Three plus four.  The answer lands in \reg{X}, the bottom line.  The tiny \reg{S} over
    \reg{L} at the top right is the simulator's stack-lift indicator; real hardware carries the battery
    gauge there instead.}

    The sum appeared without your saying where to put it.  That is the whole of RPN: intermediate results
    wait on the stack and are consumed by the next operation.

    Now the display.  Open the display menu with \fkey{DISP}.  The first row offers the six formats, and
    \mkey{FIX} takes a digit count.

    \seq{\fkey{DISP}\quad\mkey{FIX}\quad\key{4}}{X:\ \ 7.0000}

    \shotpair{03-disp}{04-mode}{Left, the first page of \fkey{DISP}: the formats on the unshifted row, the
    fraction settings on the f row above, and \mkey{IPART}, \mkey{RADIX} and \mkey{FPART} on the g row.
    Right, the first page of \fkey{MODE} for comparison.  A label with a line struck through it, like
    \mkey{DMCP} and \mkey{ActUSB} here, is a function the build you are running does not offer.}

    \begin{tip}
    Menu paging wraps.  A single press of \key{$\downarrow$} from page 1 lands on the last page, which is
    usually quicker than pressing \key{$\uparrow$} twice.
    \end{tip}

    Three things make a tutorial section work.  Every keystroke line is complete, so a reader can follow it
    without inferring a missing \key{ENTER}.  Every screen shown is a real capture of exactly those
    keystrokes.  And each step says what happened, not just what to press.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Program Entry}
\label{progentry}

    A program is given twice: once as a listing, so the reader can check what they have, and once as the
    keystrokes that produce it.  Both, always.  A listing alone leaves the reader guessing where \cmd{MVAR}
    lives; keystrokes alone give them no way to find the step they mistyped.

    The listing goes in a \cmd{prog} block, in the display face, with the four-digit step numbers the
    calculator itself shows:

    \prog{0001: LBL 'HYP'\\0002: \ \ MVAR 'a'\\0003: \ \ MVAR 'b'\\0004: \ \ RCL 'a'\\0005: \ \ x²\\0006: \ \ RCL 'b'\\0007: \ \ x²\\0008: \ \ +\\0009: \ \ √x\\0010: END}

    Then the keystrokes, in a \cmd{keying} block (Section \ref{keying}), since a program keyed in is
    exactly the sequence prose cannot carry:

    \begin{keying}
        \step{\key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.}}{open a new program}
        \step{\fkey{PRGM}}{enter program-entry mode}
        \step{\gkey{LBL}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{H}\,\key{Y}\,\key{P}\,\key{ENTER}}{the label}
        \step{\mkey{P.FN1}\ \mkey{P.FN2}\ \mkey{MVAR}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{a}\,\key{ENTER}}{declare the
            first variable; the menu stays on \menu{P.FN2}}
        \step{\mkey{MVAR}\,\fkey{$\alpha$}\,\key{b}\,\key{ENTER}}{and the second}
        \step{\ldots}{the body, straight from the listing}
        \step{\fkey{PRGM}}{leave program-entry mode}
    \end{keying}

    \begin{note}
    \cmd{MVAR} is not on \fkey{ADV}, which is where readers look for it.  In program-entry mode press
    \mkey{P.FN1} on soft key 6, then \mkey{P.FN2} on soft key 6 again, then \mkey{MVAR} on soft key 4.  Say
    this in full every time; it is the single step readers most often fail to find.
    \end{note}

    \cmd{prog} also takes anything else that wants the display face over several lines: a matrix as the editor
    shows it, a menu dump, a two-line result.  Rows are separated with \lit|\\| and columns lined up with
    \lit|\ |, since the box has no tabbing.

    \prog{y\ \ \ \ =\ \ 2.1298079\\u(y) =\ \ 0.0077526}

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Screen Captures}
\label{shots}

    \subsection{The rule}

    Every capture in a note is a real capture from the simulator, driven by the same keystrokes the note
    prints, started with \cmd{--reset} so it reproduces on any machine.  Nothing is mocked up and nothing is
    retouched.  The only processing is a threefold point upscale, which keeps the pixels square in print.

    This is not a style preference.  A note whose pictures were drawn by hand will drift away from the
    firmware silently, and the reader has no way to tell.  A note whose pictures are captures can be
    regenerated and diffed.

    \subsection{Placing one}

    Three macros, by how the picture is used.

    \begin{table}[ht]
        \caption{Capture macros}
        \label{tab:shotmacros}
        \centering
        \footnotesize
        \begin{tabular}{@{}l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.50\textwidth}@{}}
            \toprule
            \hd{Source} & \hd{Use} \\
            \midrule
            \lit|\shot[w]{file}{caption}|      & One capture in the flow of the text, centred, not floating,
                                                 so it stays where the prose put it.  Width defaults to 0.58
                                                 of the text block. \\
            \lit|\shotpair{l}{r}{caption}|     & Two side by side.  Each lands at half the measure, so a
                                                 pair is for comparing the shape of two screens, not for
                                                 reading labels off them. \\
            \lit|\shotfig[w]{f}{cap}{label}|   & A numbered float, for a capture the note cross-refers to
                                                 from elsewhere. \\
            \bottomrule
        \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    All three prepend \file{screens/} to the filename and supply the extension, so the argument is bare:
    \lit|02-result|, not the path and not the \lit|.png|.

    Prefer \cmd{shot}.  A capture belongs beside the keystrokes that produced it, and a float will wander.
    Reach for \cmd{shotfig} only when the note genuinely cross-refers to the picture, as Figure
    \ref{fig:home} is referred to from Section \ref{xref}.

    \shotfig{01-home}{A new calculator, straight out of a reset: an empty stack and MyMenu along the bottom.
    This is what every capture recipe in \file{screens/scripts} starts from.}{fig:home}

    Captions are sentences, in \cmd{soft} grey, and they say what to look at.  "The DISP menu" is not a
    caption.  "The formats on the unshifted row, the fraction settings on the f row above" is.

    \subsection{Reproducing them}

    Record the recipe.  Every note that carries captures carries a \file{screens/scripts/README.md} giving
    the build used, the exact command per picture, and the upscale step.  The pattern:

\begin{lstlisting}
./c47 --reset --exec 'press "@k 30"; press "@k 12"; press "@k 23";
                      press "@k 36"; snap s2add'
magick s2add.bmp -filter point -resize 300% screens/02-result.png
\end{lstlisting}

    \cmd{press "@k NN"} presses physical key NN, numbered row-major from the C47 table in
    \file{src/c47/assign.c}.  \cmd{press @f} and \cmd{press @g} set a pending shift, and \cmd{press F1} to
    \cmd{press F6} press the soft keys.  A longer sequence goes in a \file{.t47} script beside the README
    rather than on the command line.

    \begin{warning}
    A plot needs \cmd{--snapskiprefresh}, or \cmd{snap} redraws the screen and wipes the drawn pixels.  The
    simulator also shows a stack-lift indicator at the top right where hardware shows the battery gauge, so
    say so in the caption the first time a capture appears in a note.
    \end{warning}

    To check a capture set still reproduces after the firmware has moved, rerun the recipe and diff:

\begin{lstlisting}
compare -metric AE /tmp/regenerated.png screens/02-result.png null:
\end{lstlisting}

    Zero differing pixels is the answer you want.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Callout Blocks}
\label{callouts}

    Three kinds, and three is the whole set.  A page carrying six kinds of coloured box carries none, because
    the reader has stopped distinguishing them.

    \begin{note}
    An aside the reader can skip without losing the thread.  Background, a cross reference, a reason
    something is the way it is.
    \end{note}

    \begin{tip}
    A shorter route to the same place.  A shortcut, a default worth changing, a keystroke that saves three.
    \end{tip}

    \begin{warning}
    A trap that will cost the reader an hour.  Something that looks right and is not, a step that silently
    does nothing, or behaviour that differs between the simulator, the DM42, the DM42n and the R47.  Spend
    these sparingly; a note with nine warnings has none.
    \end{warning}

    They are unfilled, bounded on the left only, and titled in a letterspaced label.  A rule and a label are
    enough to set a paragraph apart, and they leave the page quieter than a shaded panel does.  A note and a
    tip carry the same black rule and are told apart by the title alone; only the warning spends the accent,
    because only the warning is a safety signal.

    They break across pages, so a long one is safe.  Keep them to a paragraph or two even so: a callout the
    size of a section should be a section.

    The rule for choosing is what the reader loses by skipping it.  Nothing, and it is a note.  Time, and it
    is a tip.  An hour, and it is a warning.

    There is no separate block for hardware differences, and that is deliberate.  A feature the reader's build
    does not carry, a memory ceiling, a capture that will not reproduce: each of those costs an hour, which is
    the definition of a warning.  Name the build in the first sentence and the reader knows whether the box is
    about them.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Code and Text Listings}
\label{listings}

    Two listing styles, both unfilled and closed top and bottom by the same 0.4 pt hairline a capture frame
    uses.  A listing reads as a tabulated block rather than as a shaded panel, which is what keeps it distinct
    from the \cmd{prog} and \cmd{screen} boxes: those are washed with \cmd{lcdbg} because they reproduce the
    display, and nothing else on the page is.

    \cmd{c47text} is the default and takes anything plain: a state-file excerpt, a \file{.t47} script, a
    shell command, a menu dump.

\begin{lstlisting}
EQUATIONS
1
p$\times$V=n$\times$R$\times$T
OTHER_CONFIGURATION_STUFF
\end{lstlisting}

    \cmd{mathescape} is on, so \lit|$\times$| inside a listing renders as the multiplication sign the
    calculator uses.  That is how to quote a formula containing a glyph the keyboard has but ASCII does not.

    \cmd{c47code} adds C syntax highlighting and line numbers, for firmware source:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=c47code]
/* The relative step assumes the model varies on the scale of x. */
static real_t centralDifference(real_t (*f)(real_t), real_t x) {
  const real_t h = fmax(fabs(x), 1.0) * CBRT_EPSILON;
  return (f(x + h) - f(x - h)) / (2.0 * h);
}
\end{lstlisting}

    Quote source sparingly and quote it exactly.  A note is not the place to paraphrase the firmware, and a
    listing that has drifted from the tree is worse than no listing.  Give the path with \cmd{file}, as in
    \file{src/mathematics/uncertainty.c}, so a reader can go and look.

    For one inline fragment use \cmd{lit}, which is \lit|\lit| followed by the text between two matching
    delimiters.  It is the right tool for a macro name, a flag, or a line of a file format.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Tables}
\label{tables}

    Tables are booktabs throughout: \lit|\toprule| at the top, one \lit|\midrule| under the head,
    \lit|\bottomrule| at the bottom.  Never a vertical rule and never a double rule, because both of them
    draw the eye to the grid instead of to the numbers.  \lit|@{}| on the outer columns is what makes the
    rules line up with the text block.  Small or footnote size, since a table in body size beside Helvetica
    prose looks loud.  Caption above, labelled \lsc{table} and numbered.

    Head cells go in \lit|\hd|, which letterspaces them, so the head reads as furniture without needing a
    rule or a weight change of its own.  To group rows, use \lit|\addlinespace|, never another rule: the
    palette in Table \ref{tab:palette} is grouped that way.

    \cmd{ntable} wraps the whole pattern up, and it is the shortest way to a correct table:

\begin{lstlisting}
\begin{ntable}{@{}l l@{}}{The caption sentence.}{tab:label}
    \hd{Source} & \hd{Means} \\ \midrule
    \lit|\key{7}| & Press that key \\
\end{ntable}
\end{lstlisting}

    \begin{ntable}{@{}l l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.42\textwidth}@{}}{The shape of a house table.
             Three rules, no verticals, head letterspaced.}{tab:shape}
        \hd{Column} & \hd{Type} & \hd{Notes} \\ \midrule
        First   & \lit|l|                 & Left, natural width \\
        Second  & \lit|l|                 & Left, natural width \\
        Third   & \lit|p{0.42\textwidth}| & Fixed width, so prose wraps rather than pushing the table past
                                             the margin \\
    \end{ntable}

    Give every table a caption and a label and refer to it in the prose.  A table nobody points at is
    decoration.  When a table runs wide, cut \cmd{tabcolsep} to 3 pt and drop to \cmd{footnotesize} before
    you consider landscape.

    Command reference tables are their own case, and AN0030 is the model: one small table per command, with
    rows for what it takes from the stack, what it leaves, what it reads, what it writes, and what it refuses.
    A reader looking up a command wants the same five rows every time, in the same order.


% ===================================================================================================
\section{Charts}
\label{charts}

    Charts are \cmd{pgfplots}, wrapped in \cmd{nchart} so they float and caption like any other figure.
    The chrome is recessive by default: axis lines on the left and bottom only, no box, horizontal grid
    only, ticks outside, labels in \cmd{soft}.  What is left is the data.

    \subsection{Why a chart here has almost no colour}

    This is worth the space, because the conclusion is not a preference.  A palette that encodes identity
    has to clear four things at once: a chroma floor, so a hue does not read as grey; a lightness band; a
    contrast floor against the paper; and a separation floor, measured pairwise, of $\Delta E \ge 15$ in
    normal vision and $\ge 8$ under simulated protanopia and deuteranopia.

    Rule 1 of Section \ref{palette} has already spent orange on the f shift and blue on the g shift, and
    \cmd{warnink} has spent red.  Testing what is left against those three reserved inks:

    \begin{ntable}{@{}l l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.40\textwidth}@{}}{Every hue that could have
             been a second series, and what stopped it.  Measured pairwise against \cmd{warnink},
             \cmd{fcol} and \cmd{gcol}.}{tab:charthue}
        \hd{Candidate} & \hd{Verdict} & \hd{Why} \\ \midrule
        violet \cmd{8A5CC7} & unusable & Collapses against \cmd{gcol}, $\Delta E$ 3.4 under protanopia \\
        ochre \cmd{8A6B00}  & unusable & Collapses against \cmd{warnink}, $\Delta E$ 2.5 under
                                        deuteranopia \\
        teal \cmd{00918A}   & unusable & Chroma 0.089, below the 0.10 floor: reads as grey \\
        teal \cmd{00A099}   & passes   & Clears \cmd{warnink}, \cmd{fcol} and \cmd{gcol} on every pair,
                                        in normal vision and under all three simulated deficiencies \\
    \end{ntable}

    Exactly one hue survives.  It is \cmd{chartink}, and a chart gets at most that one, for the single
    series that is the whole point of the chart.  Everything else is \cmd{keyink} and the grey ramp.

    \begin{note}
    That is not a hardship.  A note that will be printed, photocopied and read on a grey electronic-paper
    screen wants its series told apart by dash pattern and marker anyway, which is also the only encoding
    that survives a reader with full colour blindness.  The constraint and the right answer coincide.
    \end{note}

    Magnitude uses the grey ramp \cmd{seq1} to \cmd{seq5}, which passes the ordinal checks: monotone
    lightness, an adjacent step of at least $\Delta L = 0.06$, and a light end still clearing 2:1 against
    white.  \cmd{seq1} is \cmd{keyink} and \cmd{seq3} is \cmd{soft}, so the ramp is built out of the
    palette rather than beside it.

    \begin{center}
    \small\swatch{seq1}\,\swatch{seq2}\,\swatch{seq3}\,\swatch{seq4}\,\swatch{seq5}\qquad
    {\color{soft}the magnitude ramp}\qquad\swatch{chartink}\qquad{\color{soft}\cmd{chartink}}
    \end{center}

    \subsection{Emphasis: one bar is the point}

    The commonest chart in this collection ranks contributions and says which one to attack.  That is not
    a categorical chart; it is an \emph{emphasis} chart.  One mark in full \cmd{keyink}, the rest in
    \cmd{seq4}, and the reader's eye goes to the right place without a legend, a hue or a callout.

    \begin{nchart}{The GUM Annex H.1 uncertainty budget, ranked.  $l_S$ carries 62 \% of the variance, so
             it is the only term worth attacking; everything below 10 \% is noise.  Emphasis, not colour,
             is what says so.}{fig:budget}
    \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[canaxis, canbars, height=0.30\textwidth,
        xlabel={index $h_i$, \% of the combined variance},
        xmin=0, xmax=72, symbolic y coords={$\delta_\alpha$,$d$,$\delta\theta$,$l_S$},
        ytick={$\delta_\alpha$,$d$,$\delta\theta$,$l_S$}, y=15pt, enlarge y limits=0.28,
        nodes near coords align={horizontal}]
      \addplot[canrest] coordinates {(0.8,$\delta_\alpha$) (9.4,$d$) (27.7,$\delta\theta$)};
      \addplot[canemph] coordinates {(62.2,$l_S$)};
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \end{nchart}

    Sort by magnitude, always, unless the category order carries meaning of its own.  An unsorted ranking
    makes the reader do the sorting.

    \subsection{One series: the plain case}

    Most appnote charts have one series, and a single series needs no legend, because the caption names
    it.  Label the axes, give the units, and stop.

    \begin{nchart}{The wave packet $f(x) = \sin(5x)\,e^{-x^{2}/8}$ of Application Note 29
             \cite{AN0029}, over $-5 \le x \le 5$.  One series, so no legend: the caption names
             it.}{fig:wave}
    \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[canaxis, height=0.32\textwidth, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$f(x)$},
        xmin=-5, xmax=5, ymin=-1.15, ymax=1.15, ytick={-1,-0.5,0,0.5,1}]
      \addplot[canA, samples=300, domain=-5:5] {sin(deg(5*x))*exp(-x^2/8)};
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \end{nchart}

    \subsection{Two or three series: dash, then marker}

    Identity runs A, B, C in a fixed order and never cycles: \cmd{canA} solid, \cmd{canB} dashed,
    \cmd{canC} dotted, all in \cmd{keyink}.  A legend appears as soon as there is more than one series.
    A fourth series is not a fourth dash pattern; it is a second chart, or a small multiple.

    \begin{nchart}{The same packet against the envelope that bounds it, $\pm e^{-x^{2}/8}$.  Solid, dashed
             and dotted in that fixed order, one ink, and a legend because there is more than one
             series.}{fig:envelope}
    \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[canaxis, height=0.32\textwidth, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$f(x)$},
        xmin=-5, xmax=5, ymin=-1.15, ymax=1.15, ytick={-1,-0.5,0,0.5,1}]
      \addplot[canA, samples=300, domain=-5:5] {sin(deg(5*x))*exp(-x^2/8)};
      \addlegendentry{$\sin(5x)\,e^{-x^{2}/8}$}
      \addplot[canB, samples=120, domain=-5:5] {exp(-x^2/8)};
      \addlegendentry{$+e^{-x^{2}/8}$}
      \addplot[canC, samples=120, domain=-5:5] {-exp(-x^2/8)};
      \addlegendentry{$-e^{-x^{2}/8}$}
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \end{nchart}

    Measured data and a fitted curve are a special case of two series that needs no dash ladder, because
    points and a line are already distinct: use \cmd{canpoints} for the data and \cmd{canA} for the fit.

    \begin{nchart}{The five pairs of Application Note 29 \cite{AN0029} and the line the regression menu
             fits to them, $y = 2x + 1$, with $r = 1$.  Points against a line need no dash
             ladder.}{fig:fit}
    \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[canaxis, height=0.30\textwidth, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
        xmin=0, xmax=6.6, ymin=0, ymax=14.5]
      \addplot[canA, domain=0.6:6.4] {2*x+1};
      \addlegendentry{$\hat{y} = 2x + 1$}
      \addplot[canpoints] coordinates {(1,3) (2,5) (3,7) (4,9) (5,11)};
      \addlegendentry{measured}
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
    \end{nchart}

    \subsection{The rules, in short}

    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textbf{Never a second $y$ axis.}  Two measures of different scale are two charts, or one
              chart with both series indexed to a common base.  This is the single commonest way to
              mislead a reader with a chart that is technically accurate.
        \item \textbf{Identity by dash and marker, in the fixed order A, B, C.}  At most one hue,
              \cmd{chartink}, and only for the series that is the point.
        \item \textbf{Magnitude by the grey ramp}, light to dark, more is darker.  Never a rainbow.
        \item \textbf{Emphasis before category.}  If the story is "this one dominates", that is one dark
              mark among grey ones, not five hues.
        \item \textbf{Sort by magnitude} unless the category order means something.
        \item \textbf{Label the axes and give the units.}  A number without a unit is not a result.
        \item \textbf{No legend for one series}; a legend for two or more, and direct labels where they
              fit.  Never a value on every point.
        \item \textbf{Recessive chrome.}  Horizontal grid only, no box, no fill under a line unless the
              area is the quantity.
    \end{itemize}

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Mathematics}
\label{math}

    Inline mathematics sits in the sentence: the standard uncertainty of an input estimate $x_i$ is written
    $u(x_i)$, with $\nu_i = n - 1$ degrees of freedom.  Display mathematics that is not referred to again
    goes unnumbered,

    \[ u(\bar{q}) = \frac{s(q)}{\sqrt{n}} \]

    and anything the prose comes back to gets a number and a label:

    \begin{equation}
        u_c^2(y) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} c_i^2\, u^2(x_i)
                 + 2\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\sum_{j=i+1}^{N} c_i\, c_j\, u(x_i, x_j)
        \label{eq:lpu}
    \end{equation}

    Equation \ref{eq:lpu} is the law of propagation of uncertainty, and the reference is how the reader gets
    back to it twelve pages later.  Number what you cite and nothing else.

    Where a derivation matters, give it.  Where only the result matters, give the result and cite the source.
    A note that reproduces four pages of a standard the reader can download has spent four pages badly.

    \begin{align}
        s(q)   &= \sqrt{\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\left(q_k - \bar{q}\right)^2} \\
        u_c(y) &= \sqrt{\sum_i c_i^2 u^2(x_i)}
    \end{align}

    Units are set upright and separated from the number: 31.71 nm, 50.000838 mm, 0.029 $^\circ$C.  Percent
    takes a thin space, 95 \%, which is the convention the GUM uses and the collection follows.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Semantic Markup}
\label{semantic}

    Mark up what a thing \emph{is}, not how it should look.  Then a later decision about how commands should
    be set is one edit in the style file instead of a hundred in the notes.

    \begin{table}[ht]
        \caption{Semantic macros}
        \label{tab:semantic}
        \centering
        \small
        \begin{tabular}{@{}l l p{0.40\textwidth}@{}}
            \toprule
            \hd{Source} & \hd{Result} & \hd{For} \\
            \midrule
            \lit|\cmd{U.CALC}| & \cmd{U.CALC} & A command as the catalog names it \\
            \lit|\vr{U.TAB}|   & \vr{U.TAB}   & A named variable \\
            \lit|\reg{X}|      & \reg{X}      & A stack register or a labelled display field \\
            \lit|\menu{UNCT}|  & \menu{UNCT}  & A menu \\
            \lit|\file{src/c47}| & \file{src/c47} & A path in the source tree \\
            \lit|\swatch{fcol}| & \swatch{fcol} & A palette chip, outlined so a pale fill still reads \\
            \lit|\lsc{note}|    & \lsc{note}    & A letterspaced label: the one typographic accent \\
            \lit|\HW{timing}|  & \HW{timing}  & A figure still to be read off real hardware \\
            \lit|\TODO{H.2}|   & \TODO{H.2}   & A hole in the draft \\
            \bottomrule
        \end{tabular}
    \end{table}

    \cmd{HW} and \cmd{TODO} print red on purpose.  A draft circulated for comment should make its holes
    impossible to miss, and a note must not reach master with either still in it.  Grep for both before you
    open the merge request.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Cross References and Citations}
\label{xref}

    Label anything you point at and point at it by number, never by position.  "Section \ref{callouts}" and
    "Figure \ref{fig:home}" survive an edit; "the section above" and "the figure on page 4" do not.

    The label prefixes the collection uses: bare words for sections, as in \lit|\label{callouts}|, and
    \lit|fig:|, \lit|tab:| and \lit|eq:| for the three numbered kinds.

    Cite the other notes.  A note that stands alone duplicates one that already exists, and the collection is
    worth more as a set of cross references than as thirty separate documents.  Cite by number in the prose,
    as in Application Note 29 \cite{AN0029}, and give the full title in the bibliography.  External standards
    get a full entry with a URL where one exists \cite{GUM}.

    \begin{tip}
    Before writing a new note, read the ones next to it.  AN0029 was nearly filed as redundant with AN0028
    until the question was settled: AN0028 tours the settings for a reader who already has opinions about
    them, and carries no keystrokes or captures at all, while AN0029 starts at the first switch-on.  They
    cite each other now.  That conversation belongs in the note's opening paragraphs.
    \end{tip}

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Back Matter}
\label{backmatter}

    In order: any appendices, the keyboard maps if the note gives keystrokes for both machines, the
    bibliography, then the author block.

    \cmd{keyboardappendix} emits both maps, each on its own page, labelled \lit|fig:ckbd| and
    \lit|fig:rkbd| so the body can refer to them.  Ninety lines of TikZ used to be pasted into each note that
    wanted them; now it is one command.  Include it in a note that gives keystrokes, and leave it out of one
    that is purely about the firmware.  \cmd{ckeyboard} and \cmd{rkeyboard} place a single map if you want
    to lay out the page yourself.

    \cmd{colophon} takes the firmware version the note was written against and emits a closing page
    recording what built the document: the three faces and where they come from, the toolchain, the
    style-file version, and how the captures were made.  A note that promises its captures reproduce has
    to say what they reproduce against, and this is where that promise is recorded.

    The author block is \lit|\authorblock{name}{site}{email}| and carries the GFDL notice with it.  Every note
    in the collection is under the GNU Free Documentation License, and both the SPDX line at the top of the
    source and this block at the bottom are required.

    The site and the email are each optional.  Leave one or both empty and the block prints what it was
    given, so an author who has published neither is named and nothing more, which is how AN0023 credits
    its author.  Never fill either field in on somebody's behalf: a contact detail in a published document
    is theirs to offer.

    A note with more than one contributor uses \cmd{authors} instead, one \cmd{contributor} per line, in
    the order the work appears in the document.  The fourth argument is the part that is theirs, and it is
    not optional: attribution that does not say who wrote what leaves every reader guessing, and it is the
    one thing a reader cannot work out for themselves.

\begin{lstlisting}
\authors{
    \contributor{William Gordon Rutherdale}{}{}{This note}
    \contributor{Jaco Mostert}{}{}{Annex 1, the worked tutorial}
}
\end{lstlisting}

    Reach for it whenever the published PDF has more than one hand in it, including the case where the
    \file{.ltx} is one author's and an annex bound behind it is another's.  The initials in the PDF
    filename and the lines in this block have to name the same people: Section \ref{filename}.

    \begin{note}
    The first two lines of every source file are the SPDX identifier and the copyright line, then a comment
    saying what the note is and how to build it.  Copy them from this file; they are what the licence audit
    reads.
    \end{note}

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Summary}

    \begin{itemize}
        \item Copy this directory, rename the \file{.ltx} per Section \ref{filename}, edit the four
              front-matter lines, delete the prose.
        \item Check the author initials against Table \ref{tab:initials}.  There are two Davids.
        \item Build twice with \lit|TEXINPUTS=../style: lualatex|, under lualatex, with JuliaMono installed.
        \item \cmd{fcol} orange means the f shift and \cmd{gcol} blue means the g shift.  Nothing else may
              use them.
        \item Check \file{src/c47/softmenus.c} before drawing a soft key, and use \cmd{mkey}, \cmd{fmkey} or
              \cmd{gmkey} to match the row.  Eighteen items to a page, six per row.
        \item Every capture is a real \cmd{--reset} capture of the keystrokes printed beside it, with the
              recipe recorded in \file{screens/scripts/README.md}.
        \item Give a program as a listing and as keystrokes.  Both.
        \item Three callout kinds, chosen by what the reader loses in skipping the box.  A build difference
              is a warning, named by build in the first sentence.
        \item Charts tell series apart by dash and marker, in the fixed order A, B, C.  One hue is
              available, \cmd{chartink}, and exactly one: Table \ref{tab:charthue} shows what stopped
              the others.  Never a second $y$ axis.
        \item Label and cite everything you point at, including the other notes.
        \item Grep for \cmd{TODO} and \cmd{HW} before opening the merge request.
    \end{itemize}

